The '''ideosphere''' is a metaphor for the environment in which ideas circulate, interact, change and compete for attention. It is sometimes discussed in connection with memetics and cultural evolution. It is not an independently established physical layer of reality.

== Conceptual boundaries and historical context ==
The term is used loosely in discussions of idea transmission and memetics, and does not have a single agreed formal definition. Dawkins's meme analogy in ''The Selfish Gene'' (1976) is influential background for such discussions. Cultural evolutionary research supplies more explicitly testable models of transmission. The ideosphere should be distinguished from Lotman's semiosphere (cultural sign processes), Floridi's infosphere (informational environment), and a concrete knowledge graph (explicit records and relations). These are related conceptual perspectives, not synonymous objects.

== A bounded transmission model ==
Suppose p_i denotes the observed fraction of sampled participants expressing an explicitly operationalized idea variant i. A simple selection-like update can be written <math>p_i'=p_i w_i/\sum_j p_jw_j</math>, where w_i is a fitted relative transmission factor. This model assumes a closed set of variants and a specified observation interval. It is not a general law of the ideosphere: ideas can be recombined, independently invented, strategically promoted or interpreted differently by different people.

== Evidence and limitations ==
Study a delimited corpus with timestamps, citations and documented channels. Define variant equivalence and distinguish copies from independent use; test transmission hypotheses against a model including common external causes. Popularity is not truth or utility. Cultural evolution may be investigated empirically without accepting memetics as a universal theory, and an analogy between biological selection and idea circulation does not establish consciousness or agency of ideas themselves.

